title: Exactly two
mode: vhdl
chapter: Combining gates
check: behaves
---- intro
The majority circuit is 1 when **at least** two inputs are 1. Now make it 1 for **exactly** two: two yes, three no.

That is "at least two, and not all three". Signals make it readable: give each half a name, then join them.
---- task
Make `y` 1 when exactly two of `a`, `b` and `c` are 1, using the two signals.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity exactly2 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    c : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end exactly2;

architecture rtl of exactly2 is
    signal most, all3 : std_logic;
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity exactly2 is
  port(
    a : in  std_logic;
    b : in  std_logic;
    c : in  std_logic;
    y : out std_logic
  );
end exactly2;

architecture rtl of exactly2 is
    signal most, all3 : std_logic;
begin
    most <= (a and b) or (a and c) or (b and c);
    all3 <= a and b and c;
    y    <= most and not all3;
end rtl;
---- hint1
One line for `most` (the majority), one for `all3`, one for `y`.
---- hint2
`most <= (a and b) or (a and c) or (b and c);`, `all3 <= a and b and c;` and `y <= most and not all3;`
---- solution
```
most <= (a and b) or (a and c) or (b and c);
all3 <= a and b and c;
y    <= most and not all3;
```
---- done
Two pieces you already knew, joined with one more gate. Big circuits are mostly small circuits with names.
